{% decision_card number="ADR-0015" status="approved" %}
Repository control model approved 2026-07-27. External activation is tracked separately.
{% /decision_card %}

## Context

Beacon had a deployable Astro and Cloudflare Worker application but no repository-native PR controls, supply-chain policy, environment promotion definition, incident runbooks, or verified security gate. The supplied 10/10 architecture defines a secure delivery target. Repository implementation must remain honest about controls that require GitHub or Cloudflare configuration.

## Decision drivers

- protect credentials and contact-form data;
- make automated and AI-authored changes reviewable;
- prevent untrusted PR code from receiving deployment authority;
- promote the exact tested artifact;
- preserve evidence without secrets or PII;
- keep Phase 1 free of speculative backend infrastructure;
- support safe rollback and incremental control activation.

## Options considered

- continue direct manual deployment;
- use one workflow for test and production with broad credentials;
- adopt separated PR gates, protected main, immutable artifacts, environment approvals, verification, and rollback;
- introduce a third-party CI/CD platform.

## Decision

Adopt the separated control model. Repository workflows use default-deny permissions, immutable Action pins, bounded jobs, explicit quality and security gates, manual environment-gated promotion, artifact digest verification, and post-deploy checks. Canonical Markdoc records policy, threat model, exceptions, runbooks, and activation state.

No external repository setting, credential, deployment, push, or merge is authorized by this ADR. Those changes require a deliberate owner action following the activation checklist.

{% architecture_diagram title="Beacon secure CI/CD architecture" src="/diagrams/exports/beacon-secure-cicd-architecture-static.svg" source="/diagrams/source/BEACON_SECURE_CICD_ARCHITECTURE_10_OF_10.excalidraw" alt="Secure CI/CD architecture covering pull-request gates, protected main, artifact promotion, environment approval, production verification, rollback, and audit evidence" caption="The canonical local source and rendered review view referenced by ADR-0015." /%}

[Review the focused Mermaid control flow and architecture explanation](/docs/architecture/secure-cicd-architecture/).

## Positive consequences

- PR validation and deployment authority are separated;
- supply-chain inputs and direct dependencies are machine checked;
- the same tested archive can move through environments;
- runtime input and response controls are stronger;
- gaps are explicit, owned, and expiring;
- agents operate under documented least privilege.

## Negative consequences

- more CI time and maintenance;
- browser downloads and CodeQL add cost;
- a solo operator still cannot provide independent human approval;
- external setup is required before workflows can deploy;
- temporary CSP exceptions remain until preserved inline markup is refactored.

## Risks

- required-check names may drift;
- an Action pin can become outdated without review;
- manual deployment input can be wrong;
- external environment settings can differ from documentation;
- transitive vulnerabilities may remain until triaged;
- best-effort in-memory duplicate damping is not a distributed rate limit.

## Follow-up work

- activate GitHub ruleset, environments, secret scanning, and required checks;
- create separate least-privilege Cloudflare tokens;
- configure durable rate limiting, WAF, previews, alerts, and rollback access;
- triage current npm advisories and record any exception;
- remove inline handlers and styles before CSP exceptions expire;
- add a second accountable human reviewer when the business can support it;
- evaluate artifact attestations after repository settings support verification.

## Supersedes or superseded by

New decision; superseded by: none. It complements ADR-0010 and ADR-0012.

